{"name":"Sea Palm","hex":"#72897e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Semai."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large body of salt water.","The ocean; the continuous body of salt water covering a majority of the Earth's surface."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea"},{"term":"palm","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of various evergreen trees from the family Palmae or Arecaceae, which are mainly found in the tropics.","A branch or leaf of the palm, anciently borne or worn as a symbol of victory or rejoicing."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The inner and somewhat concave part of the human hand or other primate hand that extends from the wrist to the bases of the fingers.","The corresponding part of the forefoot of a nonprimate mammal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/palm"}],"entries":[{"title":"Postelsia","blocks":[{"text":"Postelsia palmaeformis, also known as the sea palm or palm seaweed, is a species of kelp and classified within brown algae in the SAR supergroup of eukaryotes. It is the only known species in the genus Postelsia. The sea palm is found along the western coast of North America, on rocky shores with constant waves."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Postelsia_palmaeformis_Salt_Point.jpg/330px-Postelsia_palmaeformis_Salt_Point.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Postelsia","wikidata":"Q3399824"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Alger%2C_Postelsia_palm%C3%A6formis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png/500px-Alger%2C_Postelsia_palm%C3%A6formis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Alger%2C_Postelsia_palm%C3%A6formis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png/500px-Alger%2C_Postelsia_palm%C3%A6formis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"One of Ruprecht's original drawings.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAlger%2C_Postelsia_palm%C3%A6formis%2C_Nordisk_familjebok.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG/500px-Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG/1280px-Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG/500px-Postelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Fossil specimen of Postelsiopsis.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APostelsiopsis_caput-medusae.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sea_palms%2C_Pigeon_Point.jpg/500px-Sea_palms%2C_Pigeon_Point.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3d/Sea_palms%2C_Pigeon_Point.jpg/1280px-Sea_palms%2C_Pigeon_Point.jpg?utm_sou